// Nearest-airport lookup for aircraft on the ground.
//
// The ADS-B feed says where an aircraft is, never what it is standing on, and
// no free service answers "which airport is at this point". So the question is
// resolved locally against a table of airports, which is the only honest way
// to name a stand: a callsign's filed route cannot say it — an aircraft on the
// ground is either at the origin or at the destination, and which one changes
// through the turnaround.
//
// The table is derived from OurAirports (https://ourairports.com/data/), which
// is released into the public domain. Filtered to large and medium airports
// with an ICAO code inside the region the fleet flies (see CIRCLES in
// api/states.js): 1372 rows, ~98 KB. Small airfields are left out on purpose —
// an airliner is not parked at one, and including them would only add nearer
// wrong answers.
//
// It lives here rather than under api/ because everything in api/ becomes a
// public endpoint, and rather than in the browser bundle because only a
// handful of aircraft are on the ground at a time: sending the whole table to
// every visitor to answer three questions would be the wrong trade.
//
// Row shape: [icao, iata, name, city, lat, lon].

